WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.414
- [Narrator] You might be
familiar with holograms

00:00:02.414 --> 00:00:03.247
(soft electronic music)

00:00:03.247 --> 00:00:04.420
or three dimensional light projections

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.450
from movies like Star Wars.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:07.735
- I regret that I'm unable
(dramatic music)

00:00:07.735 --> 00:00:09.987
to present my father's
request to you in person.

00:00:09.987 --> 00:00:10.820
(graphics blip softly)

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:11.653
(graphics whoosh)
- Hi, I'm Tim Draper, I'm.

00:00:11.653 --> 00:00:12.933
- [Narrator] Now, they're
(soft electronic music)

00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:14.870
popping up in workplaces,
as businesses find ways

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:18.320
to battle Zoom fatigue in
this era of remote working.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.800
Tech executives say this kind of hologram

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.289
may soon be common in conference
rooms around the world.

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:25.122
(upbeat electronic music)
- Hey Drew,

00:00:25.122 --> 00:00:25.955
can you check out these new designs,

00:00:25.955 --> 00:00:26.963
and let me know if you approach?

00:00:26.963 --> 00:00:28.030
(graphics blip softly)

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:28.863
(graphics blip)

00:00:28.863 --> 00:00:29.696
- I think the pandemic
(soft electronic music)

00:00:29.696 --> 00:00:31.810
has accelerated just the need

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:34.000
and use of this technology.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.320
It's a little easier to create

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.260
a hologram of yourself, your colleagues.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.899
- [Narrator] Along with beaming
into meetings or events.

00:00:40.899 --> 00:00:41.732
(people laugh)

00:00:41.732 --> 00:00:43.410
- Man, that's just amazing to me.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:44.243
- [Narrator] Holograms
(electronic music)

00:00:44.243 --> 00:00:47.000
are being used in fields
like healthcare, engineering,

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.190
education, gaming, and entertainment.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:51.350
- And so, we see this being a

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:54.250
stepping stone for people to get creative

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:55.696
about how they teach,

00:00:55.696 --> 00:00:56.970
whether it's on the modeling side,

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.990
whether it's on the 3D structure side,

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.530
chemical engineering,
it's a whole new universe.

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.590
- [Narrator] But hologram
technology could have limits

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:05.560
in the workplace.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.960
They also tend to have a
higher price point compared

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.210
to other types of
communication technology.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:12.880
With Zoom fatigue spreading, could this be

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:13.760
the next evolution
(audience claps)

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.180
in remote work?

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.580
How practical are holograms in the office?

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.503
And are they worth the cost?

00:01:20.604 --> 00:01:21.437
(graphics click)

00:01:21.437 --> 00:01:22.430
- Hi, I'm David Nussbaum.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.661
I'm the founder and CEO of Proto!

00:01:24.661 --> 00:01:25.494
(graphics click)
(soft music)

00:01:25.494 --> 00:01:28.060
- [Narrator] Proto is a Los
Angeles based tech company

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.603
that offers life-sized
photorealistic projections.

00:01:31.603 --> 00:01:33.920
- I'm Billy Morrison, I
play guitar for Billy Idol.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.660
- There is 30,000 lumens

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:36.910
of interior light.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:40.120
So, whatever the content
is, whether it's a 3D model,

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:43.040
or whether it's an NFT, or
whether it's a real person

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.720
materializing in before you, it'll appear

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.400
like that person is there.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.730
- [Narrator] Proto's holograms
(soft electronic music)

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:53.730
appear on a 7-foot tall booth
or a smaller 24-inch box.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.510
A user can beam in from
anywhere in the world.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:59.440
They just need a camera, a
plain backdrop, as well as

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.110
a set of speakers and
microphones to record them

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:03.330
in real time.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:07.810
- But we have also just
created an app that allows

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.290
the person to use their iPhone.

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:12.790
So in time, when an employee,

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.990
or CEO, or anybody needs
to beam into a location,

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:18.320
they don't need to have
an entire studio setup,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.560
all they need is a phone and our free app.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.820
- [Narrator] The hologram market

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:22.653
is projected to
(David speaks distantly)

00:02:22.653 --> 00:02:26.240
double in growth from
$2.7 billion in 2020,

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.140
to about $5.4 billion in 2024.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.250
This is largely driven by
the technology's wide range

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:35.200
of commercial applications.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.770
- The hologram industry
right now is made up

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.380
of both tech giants and smaller startups.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.040
So, companies from the big
tech giants like Google

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.300
and Microsoft are involved in the space,

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:45.650
as well as smaller startups.

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.570
And they each vary with
what they're offering

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:49.531
to consumers and businesses.

00:02:49.531 --> 00:02:50.364
(graphics click)

00:02:50.364 --> 00:02:51.197
- [Narrator] Right now,
(soft electronic music)

00:02:51.197 --> 00:02:53.220
Proto's hologram system is largely aimed

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:54.510
at business customers.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:58.580
Clients like Netflix,
T-Mobile, and Formula E racing

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:00.380
have used the device to communicate

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.220
between offices, or beam into trade shows,

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.095
entertainment events, and seminars.

00:03:06.095 --> 00:03:07.474
(man speaks distantly)

00:03:07.474 --> 00:03:09.964
(audience claps)

00:03:09.964 --> 00:03:10.797
(graphics click)

00:03:10.797 --> 00:03:13.530
Proto's larger device
is priced at $60,000,

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:17.530
or it can be rented for
about $25,000 per event.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.010
- We were recently at
New York Fashion Week,

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.230
where we were able to
beam in fashion models

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.040
from all over the place, into the device.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.920
They have the ability to walk around also

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.430
because we can track people.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:31.170
We're using it for financial institutions,

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:35.370
and interoffice
communication, for telecoms,

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.190
for office work.

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:38.600
- [Narrator] Meanwhile, the University

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.420
of Central Florida is using

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:43.666
Proto's hologram device
as an education tool.

00:03:43.666 --> 00:03:44.920
- Talking and walking, right?

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.610
That's something that we
do all the time, and we.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.120
- [Narrator] Medical students can interact

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.740
with both live and prerecorded projections

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:55.270
of patients with a variety
of medical conditions.

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:58.510
- It's like telehealth,
but it's a hologram health

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.850
because the person is really
standing there before you

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:04.430
with all of her shadows and reflections,

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:06.462
and volumetric effects.

00:04:06.462 --> 00:04:07.295
(graphics clicks)
- [Narrator] Other hologram

00:04:07.295 --> 00:04:08.990
firms are more focused on the

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:10.050
consumer market,
(graphics blip)

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.310
such as San Diego based, Ikin.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:15.450
Early next year, it plans
to launch a hologram device

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:18.260
that users can clip
into their mobile phone.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.310
The company says it
will be able to project

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:21.970
a transparent 3D hologram

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:24.840
of people on video conferencing calls.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.540
- It's more comfortable to look

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:29.580
at something that's spatially normalized

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:30.413
to your environment.

00:04:30.413 --> 00:04:32.360
It's also less intense light.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.240
And we retain the color saturation.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.110
We retain the resolution, the detail,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.170
but we also soften that light energy

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:41.210
to the point where now
it's one more normalized

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.130
to the environment, but
it's less harsh to the eyes.

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:44.590
So, you can actually
experience it for longer

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:46.280
without getting fatigue.

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:48.810
- [Narrator] Estimated
to cost less than $500,

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:52.260
Ikin says the device will
allow users to move media

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.940
from their phone directly
into the holographic display.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:58.890
- We refer to it as
interplay between states.

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.370
So, you have something on the 2D screen,

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:02.180
you can actually swipe it
up into the environment,

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:03.540
it'll immediately be translated.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:05.050
And then, you can swipe
it back to the phone,

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.600
which is quite cool.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.140
- [Narrator] Ikin's hologram devices

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:10.640
are currently being used
by several businesses,

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.270
including a U.S. military contractor,

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:15.610
that is using the technology to help find

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:18.708
and stock inventory in large warehouses.

00:05:18.708 --> 00:05:19.541
(graphics click)

00:05:19.541 --> 00:05:21.970
Holograms are also being
used to help aid on-the-job

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:23.930
field repair and research.

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:26.950
Microsoft's HoloLens 2 was used to create

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:30.270
a deep sea exploration vessel's
holographic laboratory.

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:32.630
Wearing the headsets allowed scientists

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:35.120
to interact with holographic diagrams,

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.400
and collaborate from labs
and offices around the world.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.840
- You have companies using
them to remotely train people

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:45.020
for jobs that require
people to really understand

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.970
how a big machine works.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:50.960
- [Narrator] Still, the often
bulky equipment and costs

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.840
associated with holograms,
means they have yet

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.630
to prove useful for daily interactions.

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:58.860
- While there are some companies
out there looking to make

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:02.270
these devices more consumer-friendly
and cheaper, right now,

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:05.600
it's still pretty outta
root for most consumers.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.510
So even if a consumer wanted
to use it with their friends

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:12.470
or family, it's a little out
of most people's price range.

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:14.890
- [Narrator] As with any
new form of technology,

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:18.140
hologram experiences
are not always seamless.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:21.300
The user experience varies
greatly depending on what type

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:22.970
of device is being used.

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.850
For instance, some are
more photo realistic,

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:28.010
while others are more cartoony.

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:29.950
Certain holograms can only work

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:32.440
in specific types of
lighting, while others

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:33.540
require access
(EDM music plays distantly)

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.650
to a strong power source.

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:38.720
- You can have holograms that
perhaps, don't render as well

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.300
in someone's computer because
of something they're using,

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:44.410
or maybe their internet is
just really bad that day.

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:46.280
And they (chuckles)
won't be able to see you

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.010
the way you're meant to be seen.

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:50.310
- [Narrator] While hologram companies push

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:52.090
for more widespread adoption,

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:55.340
growing demand is driving
millions of dollars of investments

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.870
in the tech and venture capital world.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:01.130
- You have players like
a tech giant like Google

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:03.450
starting to enter the space as well.

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:06.210
And I think you're starting
to see smaller startups

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:10.500
gain more interest, more
funding, and just actually be

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:12.930
something that we don't
talk about as something

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:13.763
in the movies.

00:07:13.763 --> 00:07:16.760
And something that we might
be able to use every day,

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:21.403
or see more often during
events, or in the workplace.

00:07:21.403 --> 00:07:25.570
(soft electronic music continues)

